Xamarin.UITest is a Visual Studio extension that allows to write automated functional UI tests for Xamarin.Forms applications. These tests run on emulators or physical devices.  In your application you have a screen where the user can enter her/his desired speed, a Start button, a Stop button, and a Speed Label which displays the real-time speed.

To write a functional UI test for this screen we will need to do the following steps:

1. Start the application on a simulator/emulator/device.
2. Ensure the speed label is empty (0) before we start typing in the speed entry.
3. Set the desired speed in the speed entry and click the Start button.
4. Wait for a specific amount of time.
5. Click the Stop button.
6. Check if the Speed label displays the correct speed (the speed that we entered in step 3).
7. If the speed displayed in step 6 is not the expected speed, you need to take a screenshot and log an error, because the test failed.

Let's assume that we want to test if the speed displayed in the speed label after stopping the movement is correct for the speed we typed in, when we set the speed to 10.0 on a simulator.

Here's a sample C# code that simulates these steps using Xamarin.UITest for a Xamarin.Forms application running on an iOS simulator (iPhone 8 plus):

```csharp
using NUnit.Framework;
using Xamarin.UITest;
using Xamarin.UITest.Queries;

namespace SL.UITests
{
    [TestFixture(Platform.iOS)]
    [TestFixture(Platform.Android)]
    [Category("UITests")]
    public class Tests
    {
        protected IApp app;
        private IAppQuery entryQuery;
        private IAppQuery stopButtonQuery;
        private IAppQuery speedLabelQuery;

        [SetUp]
        public void BeforeEachTest()
        {
            app = AppInitializer.StartApp();

            entryQuery = app.Query(c => c.Marked("speedEntry"));
            stopButtonQuery = app.Query(c => c.Marked("StopButton"));
            speedLabelQuery = app.Query(c => c.Marked("SpeedLabel"));

            // Clear the Speed Label before each test
            var currentSpeedLabelText = speedLabelQuery.First().Text;
            Assert.AreEqual("0", currentSpeedLabelText, "The Speed Label should be empty before each test.");
        }

        [Test]
        [Category("UI")]
        public void When_enteringSpeedAndStartingStopping_then_speedIsCorrect()
        {
            // Enter the desired speed (Assuming 10.0)
            entryQuery.Tap();
            entryQuery.TypeText("10");

            // Start the movement
            stopButtonQuery.Tap();

            // Time the function (5 seconds in this case) and then stop it
            System.Threading.Thread.Sleep(5000);
            stopButtonQuery.Tap();

            // Get the current speed after stopping
            var currentSpeedLabelText = speedLabelQuery.First().Text;

            // Check if speed label displays the correct speed
            Assert.AreEqual("10", currentSpeedLabelText, "The Speed displayed should match the entered speed.");
        }
    }
}
```
The test above will run on an iPhone 8 plus simulator (or another simulator if you're using Android) and will check if the displayed speed is correct after a 5-second interval, when we enter the speed of 10.

This test will fail if the speed label displays something else than 10 after the 5-second interval. In that case, you should take a screenshot, log the error, and make sure the appropriate fix is found and tested again.

